\section{Two abstractions called a virtual machine}

A language VM implements a program representation, usually inside an OS process. Its instructions can refer to managed objects and functions without specifying a CPU's privilege mechanism or device registers. A system VM instead presents enough of a machine to run a guest kernel. The kernel can manage virtual CPUs and guest resources, subject to a monitor that controls the real resources. In \emph{Architecture of Virtual Machines}, R.\ P.\ Goldberg distinguishes the map a guest sees from an underlying map that only its monitor manages. Mapping a process address in a guest to a real resource composes those maps; it does not give guest code direct authority over the second map.

Russ Cox, Frans Kaashoek, and Robert Morris's \emph{xv6: a simple, Unix-like teaching operating system} illustrates a concrete kernel boundary. On RISC-V xv6 the syscall trap starts in supervisor mode at a trampoline mapped at a common virtual address in the user and kernel page tables. The kernel saves user state and switches page tables as part of entering its handler. Compare a bytecode call: it normally changes a managed frame within the same OS process and need not cross a privilege boundary. A JIT call written as a machine \texttt{call} is no different in this respect. The kernel sees host machine state, not language object identities.

\section{Matching runtime services to OS mechanisms}

A managed heap may reserve a large contiguous address range while backing pages only as demand grows. Address space reserved for future use, committed memory under the platform's policy, and resident physical memory are different measurements. Touching fresh pages can fault even when a VM allocation is just a bounds check and a pointer increment. Conversely, reducing the live object count will not necessarily reduce resident memory unless the runtime can release unused pages. Use the OS page size for protection boundaries, not the managed object's alignment. A guard page for a native thread's stack cannot by itself enforce a managed stack bound if frames are kept in heap segments.

Native code needs a separate contract with the OS. For a platform with distinct writable and executable mappings, initialize a code region while writable and publish entry addresses only once code and metadata are complete and the region can be executed under the platform's policy. Perform the required instruction-cache synchronization if the target requires it. If no executable-memory facility is available, select an interpreter or an approved ahead-of-time image. That is a configuration decision, not a reason to assume that permission failures can be ignored.

\section{Worked boundary trace: a managed read}

Suppose an application asks its runtime to fill 4,096 bytes of a managed array from an authorized open file, with a partial count allowed on end-of-file. A blocking implementation can use a rooted array plus a stable native scratch buffer: enter the native-call state, pass the native buffer to the OS, and record the actual byte count on return. If the first read returns 1,536 bytes, continue from offset 1,536 with a remaining length of 2,560. Stop on end-of-file, cancellation, or an error according to the API contract; do not report a full transfer simply because the kernel call returned successfully. Copy the bytes actually read into the managed array once it is safe to resolve its root again. The scratch buffer cannot be returned to its pool before outstanding work has stopped using it.

A fake device test can return two short reads while a collection moves the managed array, then signal end-of-file. Assert the returned length, byte order, and lack of writes beyond the received range; the collection distinguishes proper root management from retaining a stale address. In a system VM this same host-facing path may involve both a guest syscall and a separate virtual device operation. Instrument the boundaries independently before attributing cost to the guest kernel or the monitor.

