\subsection{Account for Heap Growth in Whole OS Pages}

A managed allocation frontier and the OS backing frontier
have different units. Assume one thread owns a contiguous
reserved interval, the page size is fixed, and a runtime-specific
backing operation either makes every page in its requested
range accessible or leaves the range unchanged on failure.
This operation does not promise residency or that the first
access will be cheap. For an append-only arena with no collection
in this routine, maintain a byte cursor and a page-aligned
backed limit, both offsets from the reserved base. Require
positive request sizes and a power-of-two object alignment
that divides the page size. Do all rounding and addition with
checked arithmetic.

\begin{sudocode}[Grow a reserved arena on demand]
\State $a\gets\Call{AlignUp}{cursor,alignment}$
\State $end\gets$ checked sum of $a$ and requested bytes
\If{arithmetic failed or $end>reserved$}
  \State \Return no space \Comment{Do not touch the OS mapping}
\EndIf
\If{$end>backed$}
  \State $b\gets\Call{AlignUp}{end,pageSize}$
  \If{\Call{BackRange}{$backed,b$} fails}
    \State \Return no backing \Comment{Keep both frontiers unchanged}
  \EndIf
  \State $backed\gets b$
\EndIf
\State $cursor\gets end$
\State \Return address at offset $a$
\end{sudocode}

With 4096-byte pages, 16,384 bytes reserved, 4096 backed, and a
cursor at 4080, a 40-byte request aligned to 16 ends at 4120.
The runtime backs $[4096,8192)$ and returns offset 4080, not
4096. Inject backing failure: the cursor must stay 4080 and the
backed limit 4096. A subsequent 16-byte request can succeed
without another OS call, ending exactly at 4096.

\Cref{fig:visual-246} separates the change to the byte cursor from the page range supplied to the OS adapter.

